WEBVTT

00:00.644 --> 00:02.002
- [Voiceover] We're asked to subtract

00:02.002 --> 00:05.067
negative two x squared
plus four x minus one

00:05.067 --> 00:09.757
from six x squared plus
three x minus nine,

00:09.757 --> 00:11.406
and like always I encourage
you to pause the video

00:11.406 --> 00:13.797
and see if you can give it a go.

00:13.797 --> 00:16.096
All right, now let's work
through this together.

00:16.096 --> 00:20.020
So I could rewrite this as six x squared

00:20.020 --> 00:23.514
plus three x minus nine

00:23.514 --> 00:28.321
minus, minus this
expression right over here,

00:28.321 --> 00:29.905
so I'll put that in parentheses,

00:29.905 --> 00:34.895
minus negative two x squared,
negative two x squared

00:34.895 --> 00:39.130
plus four x minus one.

00:39.130 --> 00:40.999
Now what can we do from here?

00:40.999 --> 00:43.263
Well, we can distribute
this negative side,

00:43.263 --> 00:45.562
we can distribute this negative side,

00:45.562 --> 00:47.141
and then if we did that,

00:47.141 --> 00:50.184
we would get the six x squared
plus three x minus nine

00:50.184 --> 00:51.774
won't change so we still have that.

00:51.774 --> 00:56.464
Six x squared plus three x minus nine,

00:56.464 --> 00:59.064
but if I distribute the negative side,

00:59.064 --> 01:01.445
the negative of negative two x squared

01:01.445 --> 01:04.730
is positive two x squared.

01:04.730 --> 01:07.841
So that's going to be positive two...

01:07.841 --> 01:09.304
Get a little more space.

01:09.304 --> 01:13.274
Positive two x squared, and then subtract,

01:13.274 --> 01:15.875
and then the negative
of positive four x is...

01:15.875 --> 01:18.221
I'm going to subtract four x now,

01:18.221 --> 01:20.692
and then the negative of negative one,

01:20.692 --> 01:23.513
or the opposite of negative one
is going to be positive one.

01:23.513 --> 01:26.114
So I've just distributed
the negative side,

01:26.114 --> 01:29.910
and now I can add terms
that have the same degree

01:29.910 --> 01:31.763
on our x...

01:31.763 --> 01:33.871
The same degree terms,
I guess you could say,

01:33.871 --> 01:37.337
so I have an x squared term,

01:37.337 --> 01:38.945
here it's six x squared,

01:38.945 --> 01:41.290
here I have a two x squared term,

01:41.290 --> 01:42.660
so I can add those two together,

01:42.660 --> 01:44.401
six x squared plus two x squared.

01:44.401 --> 01:46.583
If I have six x squareds
and then I have another

01:46.583 --> 01:49.869
two x squareds, how many x
squareds am I now going to have?

01:49.869 --> 01:54.048
I'm now going to have eight
x squareds, eight x squareds,

01:54.048 --> 01:56.169
or six x squared plus two x squared.

01:56.169 --> 01:58.433
We add the coefficients,
the six and the two

01:58.433 --> 02:00.824
to get eight, eight x squared.

02:00.824 --> 02:04.324
Then we can add the x terms.

02:04.324 --> 02:06.212
You could view these as
the first-degree terms,

02:06.212 --> 02:08.120
three x...

02:08.120 --> 02:11.615
We have three x

02:11.615 --> 02:14.135
and then we have minus four x,

02:14.135 --> 02:16.189
so three x minus four x,

02:16.189 --> 02:18.244
if I have three of something
and I take away four of them,

02:18.244 --> 02:20.891
I'm now going to have
negative one of that thing,

02:20.891 --> 02:22.470
or you could say that the coefficients,

02:22.470 --> 02:24.339
three minus four would be negative one.

02:24.339 --> 02:26.730
So I now have negative one x.

02:26.730 --> 02:28.739
I could write it as negative one x,

02:28.739 --> 02:31.784
but I might as well just
write it as negative x.

02:31.784 --> 02:33.858
That's the same thing as negative one x.

02:33.858 --> 02:36.679
And then finally, I can deal
with our constant terms.

02:36.679 --> 02:40.603
I'm subtracting a nine
and then I'm adding a one.

02:40.603 --> 02:43.447
So you could say, "Well, what's
a negative nine plus one?"

02:43.447 --> 02:45.340
Well, that's going to be negative eight.

02:45.340 --> 02:49.535
That's going to be negative
eight and we are all done.

02:49.535 --> 02:52.514
And one thing that you
might find interesting

02:52.514 --> 02:56.519
is I had a polynomial here and from that

02:56.519 --> 02:58.322
I subtracted another polynomial,

02:58.322 --> 03:00.957
and notice, I got a polynomial,

03:00.957 --> 03:04.062
and this is actually always
going to be the case.

03:04.062 --> 03:07.354
If you think about the
set of all polynomials,

03:07.354 --> 03:08.550
if you just think about the set...

03:08.550 --> 03:10.536
Let me do this in a neutral color.

03:10.536 --> 03:12.022
So if we think about the set

03:12.022 --> 03:14.495
of all polynomials right over here,

03:14.495 --> 03:16.329
and if you take one polynomial,

03:16.329 --> 03:19.452
which you could imagine
this magenta polynomial.

03:19.452 --> 03:21.193
So this is a polynomial right over here,

03:21.193 --> 03:24.322
let's call this p of x.

03:24.322 --> 03:27.299
So this is p of x right over there,

03:27.299 --> 03:31.305
p of x, and then you
have another polynomial,

03:32.325 --> 03:34.929
this one right over here, let's call this,

03:34.929 --> 03:38.203
I don't know, we can call this q of x,

03:38.203 --> 03:40.443
q of x, just for kicks.

03:40.443 --> 03:43.833
So that's q of x, just like that.

03:43.833 --> 03:45.586
And if you apply the...

03:45.586 --> 03:48.693
In this case, we applied
the subtraction operator.

03:48.693 --> 03:50.450
If we apply subtraction...

03:50.450 --> 03:53.097
So you took these two,
you took these two...

03:53.097 --> 03:55.395
Let me see how I could depict this well.

03:55.395 --> 04:00.132
So we took p of x and you
subtracted from that q of x.

04:00.132 --> 04:02.561
We still get a polynomial,

04:02.561 --> 04:04.106
so that's going to give us...

04:04.106 --> 04:06.995
We stay in the set of polynomials.

04:06.995 --> 04:09.479
At any time you have a set of things,

04:09.479 --> 04:11.267
and you might be more
used to talking about this

04:11.267 --> 04:13.100
in terms of integers,

04:13.100 --> 04:16.944
or number sets, but you can
talk about this in general.

04:16.944 --> 04:18.685
Here we're talking about
the set of polynomials,

04:18.685 --> 04:21.123
and we just saw that if we
start with two polynomials,

04:21.123 --> 04:23.117
two members of the set of polynomials...

04:23.117 --> 04:25.316
Let me be clear, this is polynomials,

04:25.316 --> 04:28.245
polynomials, right over here.

04:28.245 --> 04:29.844
You take two members of the set

04:29.844 --> 04:32.534
and you perform the subtraction operation,

04:32.534 --> 04:35.695
you're still going to
get a member of the set.

04:35.695 --> 04:37.871
And when you have a situation like this,

04:37.871 --> 04:39.411
I could call this one,

04:39.411 --> 04:40.467
I don't know, I'm running out of letters.

04:40.467 --> 04:42.394
Well let me just call this one f of x,

04:42.394 --> 04:44.437
so we've got f of x here.

04:44.437 --> 04:45.746
When you have this
situation where you take

04:45.746 --> 04:48.280
two members of a set, you
apply an operator on them,

04:48.280 --> 04:50.451
or you take a certain
number of members of a set,

04:50.451 --> 04:51.391
you apply an operator on them,

04:51.391 --> 04:52.842
and you still get a member of the set,

04:52.842 --> 04:57.649
we would say that this set is
closed under that operation.

04:57.649 --> 05:00.297
So we could say that the set

05:00.297 --> 05:04.325
of polynomials, set of polynomials,

05:05.428 --> 05:08.662
polynomials closed,

05:09.863 --> 05:11.094
closed...

05:11.094 --> 05:12.188
I won't even put it in quotes.

05:12.188 --> 05:16.288
Closed under, under subtraction.

05:16.288 --> 05:19.244
And I didn't prove it here,
I just did one example

05:19.244 --> 05:21.520
where I subtracted two polynomials

05:21.520 --> 05:22.586
and I got another one,

05:22.586 --> 05:25.464
and there's clearly more
rigorous proofs that you can do.

05:25.464 --> 05:27.043
But this is actually the
case, as long as you have

05:27.043 --> 05:28.985
two polynomials, you apply subtraction,

05:28.985 --> 05:32.236
you're going to get another polynomial.

05:32.236 --> 05:35.289
And the fancy way of saying
that is the set of polynomials

05:35.289 --> 05:37.123
is closed under subtraction.

05:37.123 --> 05:39.352
This notion of closure
sometimes seems like this

05:39.352 --> 05:42.464
very fancy mathematical
idea, but it's not too fancy.

05:42.464 --> 05:43.822
It's just you take two members of a set,

05:43.822 --> 05:45.018
you apply an operation,

05:45.018 --> 05:46.515
if you still get a member of the set

05:46.515 --> 05:49.543
after that operation,
then that set is closed

05:49.543 --> 05:51.538
under that operation.
